  "summary": "The biblical pattern of holy rest that begins in creation, becomes a covenant sign in Israel, and points toward land-rest and eschatological rest.",
  "historical_context": "Sabbath emerges in creation theology and then in Israel's covenant life as a weekly sign of trust, liberation, and ordered time.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "Ancient cultures knew rest and festival calendars, but Israel's Sabbath uniquely ties rest to creation, redemption from slavery, and covenant holiness.",
  "hebraic_worldview": "Creation rhythm, memory, trust, holiness, household rest, and freedom from oppression.",
  "second_temple_context": "By the Second Temple period Sabbath observance marked covenant fidelity and shaped synagogue and household rhythms.",
  "canonical_context": "Creation rest becomes exodus remembrance, covenant sign, land-rest hope, and a pattern fulfilled in Christ and the final rest.",
  "later_christian_reception": "Christian traditions debated Sabbath and Lord's Day practice, often linking the theme to worship, mercy, and eschatological rest.",
  "literary_context": "Sabbath appears in creation narrative, law, prophets, Gospels, epistles, and apocalyptic writing as Scripture develops the theme.",
  "covenantal_significance": "Sabbath is a covenant sign that marks Israel's belonging to the LORD and shapes trustful rhythms of rest.",
